WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.440 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Two students draw different-looking angles
and both say theirs is bigger.

2
00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:07.170 line:1% position:50% size:90%
How could we settle their argument fairly?

3
00:00:08.190 --> 00:00:12.430 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Today we are learning to measure and draw
angles with a protractor.

4
00:00:13.445 --> 00:00:17.355 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Our learning goal is to measure and draw
angles with a protractor.

5
00:00:18.399 --> 00:00:23.109 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Success criteria: align the vertex, find
the zero baseline and read

6
00:00:23.109 --> 00:00:24.609 line:1% position:50% size:90%
the correct degree scale.

7
00:00:25.661 --> 00:00:30.631 line:1% position:50% size:90%
An angle is the amount of turning between
two rays meeting at a vertex.

8
00:00:30.661 --> 00:00:33.821 line:1% position:50% size:90%
The length of the rays does not change the
angle.

9
00:00:34.871 --> 00:00:39.521 line:1% position:50% size:90%
A full turn is three hundred and sixty
degrees, a half turn is one hundred

10
00:00:39.521 --> 00:00:41.991 line:1% position:50% size:90%
and eighty, and a quarter turn is ninety.

11
00:00:42.976 --> 00:00:44.536 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Look at this angle.

12
00:00:44.606 --> 00:00:47.446 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Is it acute, right, obtuse or straight?

13
00:00:47.586 --> 00:00:50.146 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Pause for five seconds and decide.

14
00:00:55.155 --> 00:01:00.195 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Sixty degrees is smaller than ninety
degrees, so this is an acute angle.

15
00:01:01.235 --> 00:01:03.895 line:1% position:50% size:90%
A right angle is ninety degrees.

16
00:01:03.905 --> 00:01:06.025 line:1% position:50% size:90%
It looks like the corner of a square.

17
00:01:07.112 --> 00:01:10.602 line:1% position:50% size:90%
An angle is one hundred and twenty
degrees.

18
00:01:10.602 --> 00:01:12.912 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Is it acute or obtuse?

19
00:01:12.912 --> 00:01:15.222 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Compare it with ninety degrees.

20
00:01:20.234 --> 00:01:24.574 line:1% position:50% size:90%
One hundred and twenty is greater than
ninety and less than one hundred and

21
00:01:24.574 --> 00:01:26.874 line:1% position:50% size:90%
eighty, so the angle is obtuse.

22
00:01:27.916 --> 00:01:31.996 line:1% position:50% size:90%
An angle with longer arms is not
necessarily larger.

23
00:01:32.056 --> 00:01:35.766 line:1% position:50% size:90%
We measure the amount of turning, not the
length of the lines.

24
00:01:36.771 --> 00:01:40.851 line:1% position:50% size:90%
When measuring with a protractor, place
the centre at the vertex and align

25
00:01:40.851 --> 00:01:43.511 line:1% position:50% size:90%
one arm with the zero-degree baseline.

26
00:01:44.537 --> 00:01:48.597 line:1% position:50% size:90%
If the angle looks narrower than a right
angle, should you read seventy-

27
00:01:48.597 --> 00:01:51.507 line:1% position:50% size:90%
five or one hundred and five degrees?

28
00:01:56.514 --> 00:02:00.934 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Seventy-five degrees is the sensible
measurement because it is less than

29
00:02:00.934 --> 00:02:02.174 line:1% position:50% size:90%
ninety degrees.

30
00:02:03.205 --> 00:02:08.135 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Always check which protractor scale begins
at zero along your starting arm.

31
00:02:08.175 --> 00:02:11.095 line:1% position:50% size:90%
The two scales count in opposite
directions.

32
00:02:12.107 --> 00:02:15.317 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Two adjacent angles lie on a straight
line.

33
00:02:15.357 --> 00:02:17.307 line:1% position:50% size:90%
One is forty degrees.

34
00:02:17.337 --> 00:02:19.157 line:1% position:50% size:90%
What is the other angle?

35
00:02:24.241 --> 00:02:27.821 line:1% position:50% size:90%
A straight angle is one hundred and eighty
degrees.

36
00:02:27.841 --> 00:02:31.661 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Subtract forty to get one hundred and
forty degrees.

37
00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:36.970 line:1% position:50% size:90%
To draw an angle, draw the first ray, mark
the required number of degrees

38
00:02:36.970 --> 00:02:39.740 line:1% position:50% size:90%
from zero and connect the mark to the
vertex.

39
00:02:40.750 --> 00:02:44.400 line:1% position:50% size:90%
A robot makes two quarter turns in the
same direction.

40
00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:47.550 line:1% position:50% size:90%
How many degrees has it turned altogether?

41
00:02:52.588 --> 00:02:57.168 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Ninety plus ninety equals one hundred and
eighty degrees, which is a half

42
00:02:57.168 --> 00:02:57.798 line:1% position:50% size:90%
turn.

43
00:02:58.815 --> 00:03:02.165 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Angles are measured in degrees, not
centimetres.

44
00:03:02.195 --> 00:03:06.675 line:1% position:50% size:90%
A small drawing and a large drawing can
show exactly the same turn.

45
00:03:07.702 --> 00:03:09.772 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Imagine a door opening.

46
00:03:09.812 --> 00:03:14.562 line:1% position:50% size:90%
A small opening makes an acute angle,
while a door opened square to the wall

47
00:03:14.562 --> 00:03:15.912 line:1% position:50% size:90%
makes a right angle.

48
00:03:16.972 --> 00:03:21.072 line:1% position:50% size:90%
A turn of thirty degrees is one third of a
right angle.

49
00:03:21.122 --> 00:03:24.295 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Three such turns add up to ninety degrees.

50
00:03:25.254 --> 00:03:29.684 line:1% position:50% size:90%
A turn of one hundred and fifty degrees is
close to a straight angle, but

51
00:03:29.684 --> 00:03:32.414 line:1% position:50% size:90%
thirty degrees short of one hundred and
eighty.

52
00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:35.570 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Use benchmarks to estimate.

53
00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:40.110 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Compare the angle with ninety degrees
before reading a protractor scale.

54
00:03:41.171 --> 00:03:45.121 line:1% position:50% size:90%
If your measurement says forty degrees but
the drawing looks wider than a

55
00:03:45.121 --> 00:03:48.021 line:1% position:50% size:90%
right angle, check whether you used the
wrong scale.

56
00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:53.340 line:1% position:50% size:90%
When drawing an angle, keep the ruler
steady and make sure both rays begin

57
00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:54.770 line:1% position:50% size:90%
at the same vertex.

58
00:03:55.759 --> 00:03:59.019 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Explain your answer using the size of the
turn.

59
00:03:59.079 --> 00:04:02.799 line:1% position:50% size:90%
This is stronger than simply saying the
angle looks big.

60
00:04:03.847 --> 00:04:08.487 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Before using a protractor, predict whether
the angle is less than or greater

61
00:04:08.487 --> 00:04:13.287 line:1% position:50% size:90%
than ninety degrees. Your prediction can
catch a wrong-scale reading.

62
00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:17.870 line:1% position:50% size:90%
The centre mark must sit exactly on the
vertex.

63
00:04:17.890 --> 00:04:22.580 line:1% position:50% size:90%
If it is moved away, the protractor
markings no longer measure the intended

64
00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:23.230 line:1% position:50% size:90%
angle.

65
00:04:24.285 --> 00:04:28.695 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Some protractors number clockwise and
anticlockwise at once.

66
00:04:28.705 --> 00:04:33.275 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Find the zero aligned with the starting
ray before following the numbers.

67
00:04:34.265 --> 00:04:36.915 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Suppose your first ray points left.

68
00:04:36.945 --> 00:04:41.345 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Begin with the zero on the left, rather
than automatically reading from the

69
00:04:41.345 --> 00:04:42.475 line:1% position:50% size:90%
right-hand scale.

70
00:04:43.526 --> 00:04:47.605 line:1% position:50% size:90%
An angle of thirty degrees is much smaller
than a right angle.

71
00:04:47.656 --> 00:04:50.756 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Use this comparison when checking your
drawing.

72
00:04:51.787 --> 00:04:56.097 line:1% position:50% size:90%
An angle of one hundred and sixty degrees
is almost straight.

73
00:04:56.247 --> 00:04:59.317 line:1% position:50% size:90%
It is twenty degrees away from a straight
angle.

74
00:05:00.382 --> 00:05:04.852 line:1% position:50% size:90%
If you draw a sixty-degree angle and
measure it again, your answer should be

75
00:05:04.852 --> 00:05:06.632 line:1% position:50% size:90%
close to sixty degrees.

76
00:05:06.632 --> 00:05:08.802 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Small pencil errors may matter.

77
00:05:09.883 --> 00:05:14.203 line:1% position:50% size:90%
For an independent check, draw an angle
measuring one hundred and ten

78
00:05:14.203 --> 00:05:18.183 line:1% position:50% size:90%
degrees. Predict its type before touching
the protractor.

79
00:05:19.224 --> 00:05:21.134 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Explain the steps to someone else:

80
00:05:21.134 --> 00:05:25.534 line:1% position:50% size:90%
estimate the angle, align the baseline,
read the correct scale and check the

81
00:05:25.534 --> 00:05:26.444 line:1% position:50% size:90%
angle type.

82
00:05:27.570 --> 00:05:31.990 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Your challenge is to find three angles in
your classroom, classify them and

83
00:05:31.990 --> 00:05:33.420 line:1% position:50% size:90%
explain your reasoning.
